    "p48b14"
   ],
   "role": "small",
   "why": "outside any article",
   "words": 189,
   "text": "‘Divine Faith’. It was an elitist, eclectic religious movement which never numbered more than 19 adherents, formulated by the Mughal emperor Akbar in the late 16th Century CE. Fiqh Islamic jurisprudence. Hadith sayings and deeds of the Prophet of Islam. Hudaibiya a place just outside Makkah where, in 628 CE, the Treaty of Hudaibiya was entered into between the Prophet Muhammad, representing the state of Madinah, and the Quraysh tribe of Makkah. Ibn Majah 9th century Islamic scholar and compiler of Hadith. Ijtihad the application of independent reasoning in the light of the Quran and Hadith to arrive at a knowledge of truth in religious matters. Imam Al-Bukhari 9th century Islamic scholar who authored the Hadith collection known as Sahih Al-Bukhari, which is regarded as the most authentic collection of Hadith. Mujtahid one who exercises Ijtihad or independent reasoning in the light of the Quran and Hadith to understand religious matters. Najd a region in the centre of the Arabian Peninsula. Sahih Al-Bukhari Hadith collection by Imam Al-Bukhari, regarded as the most authentic such collection. Salah also Salat; the Islamic prayer. Shariah moral code and religious law of Islam."
  },
